# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Env / secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Node / Next
node_modules/
.next/
out/
dist/

# Held-out grading suites must never leave the machine in the clear.
# Ignore the ENTIRE private dir (any extension, any subdir) so a .jsonl/.csv/
# .txt or nested suite can never become committable. This is the moat.
backend/data/private/

# Run artifacts / transcripts
backend/data/runs/
*.log
*.sqlite
*.db

# OS
.DS_Store

# Generated deploy wrapper for the lander (source is frontend/index.html)
frontend/standalone.html
OUTREACH_TARGETS.md
OUTREACH.md

# Local timestamped backups
*.bak
*.bak.*

# One-off / debug grading + scratch scripts — NEVER commit (they tend to carry
# hardcoded internal endpoints; this is how infra leaked before the 2026-07 clean).
# Reusable reference-cohort scripts (grade_dify.py, grade_flowise.py, ...) are fine;
# keep throwaway probes under backend/scratch/ or matching these patterns.
backend/pg_*.py
backend/grade_*_direct.py
backend/scratch/

# Generated per-agent scorecards. These contain a vendor's own transcripts and are
# sent privately, never published. The generator (app/leaderboard/report.py) IS public.
frontend/scorecards/

# Execution-sandbox run artifacts, same class as data/runs/ above: generated,
# and they carry agent transcripts.
backend/data/exec_runs/

# The FULL reproducibility record names the held-out probes whose verdicts moved,
# which is the useful thing internally and the one thing that must not be
# published: those ids come from backend/data/private/, the moat ignored above.
# A probe id reveals nothing of a probe's content and does
# reveal how many probes of a given family the held-out set holds -- the id encodes
# a family and an ordinal, so one leaked id bounds the suite from below. Deliberately
# NOT illustrated with a real id here: an earlier version of this comment quoted one,
# which published a held-out identifier inside the rule forbidding exactly that.
# The redacted companion (measurements.public.json) carries the same rates,
# bounds, drift and spend with movements reported per DIMENSION, and IS committed.
# ⛔ DENY BY DEFAULT, whitelist the redacted companions. This named ONE FILENAME,
# which is the shape aivonic-7c found across the estate on 2026-09-25: a rule that
# was right when written, whose coverage rots as the lane produces more files, and
# which is invisible to "stage what you wrote" because you DID write them. A second
# full record under any other name would have been staged by the next broad sweep.
# Now a new file here is ignored unless it is explicitly a *.public.json companion,
# so nobody has to remember.
backend/data/reproducibility/*
!backend/data/reproducibility/*.public.json

# Canary baseline records HELD-OUT probe ids (and per-judge scores) from
# data/private/. Same reason data/runs/ and data/private/ are ignored: the repo
# is public and the held-out split is what stops a vendor training on the test.
backend/data/judge_canary_baseline.json

# Reference-build RUNTIME STATE. A graded reference stack runs in place here, so
# these directories hold a live redis dump, weaviate databases and a postgres
# pgdata - the working state of a system we graded, in a PUBLIC repo. Today a
# `git add -A` aborts on permission-denied rather than committing them, which is
# luck rather than design: chown the tree or run as root and it stages. The
# curated docker-compose.pg-ref.yml stays TRACKED; it is the reproducible part.
backend/reference_agents/stacks/*/volumes/

# The GENERATED Dify compose for the reference stack. 967 lines, 69 secret-ish
# keys, and 56 of them are `${VAR:-<literal>}` - an env reference with a default
# BAKED INTO THE FILE. 35 of those defaults are substantial (>=12 chars).
#
# ⛔ I PREVIOUSLY REPORTED THIS FILE CLEAN AND THAT WAS WRONG. The check treated
# any value starting with "${" as a safe env reference, which discards exactly the
# dangerous case: the text after `:-` is a literal that ships with the file. It
# reported zero findings, and zero was the answer I was hoping for. No positive
# control was run, so nothing established the probe could find a secret at all.
#
# Whether those defaults are Dify's published placeholders or this stack's real
# credentials is UNRESOLVED: upstream has since moved these into .env, so a
# hash comparison against it returns "not comparable" rather than a verdict, and
# shape alone cannot tell a vendor default from a provisioned password. The safe
# action does not depend on the answer. Found by a third session after mine
# passed it.
#
# docker-compose.pg-ref.yml is the curated, reproducible one and stays TRACKED.
backend/reference_agents/stacks/*/docker-compose.yaml

# Withheld scorecards: a graded vendor's full card, including the agent's own
# harmful output verbatim, held back pending disclosure. This repo is PUBLIC.
# Committing one would be a worse exposure than the docroot leak that created
# this directory, because git history is not revocable.
backend/data/withheld-scorecards/
